schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "On-Chain Attestations: Your Digital Proof Record"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A beginner-level explainer of on-chain attestations as permanent,
    verifiable, tamper-proof digital proof records for skills, training
    completion, volunteering, and community impact — framed as a replacement
    for paper certificates that can be lost, damaged, or forged. Explains the
    mechanism in three steps (someone vouches for you → it's recorded
    permanently on a blockchain → anyone can verify it) and lists use cases:
    proving skills for scholarships/jobs, building a portable reputation,
    showing community impact, and accessing opportunity programs that require
    verifiable proof.
  audience: >-
    Written directly for young women participants in "Sarreya programs" (a
    named training/mentorship context referenced in the source article,
    apparently East Africa / Somaliland-adjacent based on sibling articles in
    this batch); beginner, non-technical.
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      The article asserts attestations are simply superior to paper
      certificates on every axis (permanent, verifiable, portable,
      trustworthy) without discussing failure modes (e.g. the vouching party
      itself being wrong, revoked, or unaccountable) — see accompanying
      claim-evidence entry.
  related_concepts:
    - onchain-attestation-as-vouching-record
  related_resources: []
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/onchain-attestations.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
